      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate \u2014 the DfT study (E20) is correctly labelled measurable as it reports observed outcomes. The E16 source (fletcherssolicitors.co.uk, a solicitors firm) is advocacy-adjacent but is described as citing 'a large UK evidence review'; this is a minor sourcing concern but not blocking given corroboration from institutional sources. The E9 claim (theguardian.com) similarly benefits from corroboration. The verdict correctly separates air-quality effects (O3/O6) from road-safety effects (O5), avoiding lever leakage. The 'worsens/moderate' direction is well-grounded: the weight of institutional evidence (TfL, Wales, LTN studies) supports casualty increases from removal, and the DfT counter-finding is honestly acknowledged. No party-identity language or motive attribution detected. The 'except where safety is critical' caveat is properly flagged as the biggest unknown rather than dismissed. No false balance issues \u2014 the verdict does not manufacture uncertainty; it correctly notes a genuine partial counter-finding while leaning with the stronger evidence."
